! Folded — where each slot's content went

This carrier held four measurements of another hearth's rooms, written as a handback because `docs/pono/**` stood on the meme hearth's ground and a crossing to fix a doc belongs in a handback rather than in an edit. The operator closed that hearth on 2026-09-13; the ground became the standing hearth's, and every item landed.

''The standing hearth re-verified each measurement before rewriting a word'' — the discipline this carrier itself argued for. Re-derived with its own commands: the witness fleet at ''34 globbed, 29 run'', the `witnesses` job and its roll-call assertion in `ci.yml`, `gate:fold-ts` at `ci.yml:380`, `pnpm test` at `ci.yml:160`, and the python split at ''989 + 27 = 1016 collected''. Every number held. One suggestion did NOT survive contact and the fold records it below.

|!slot |!where it went |
|#/law · #/what-the-founding-hearth-touched |folded to `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths` #/crossings — <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths">> `#/crossings` — the row //The four stale rooms, measured and NOT edited//, which carries the handback's own law (a measurement of another hearth's ground crosses by CARRIER, shipping the re-derivation beside every number) and the sweep-keyed-on-a-phrase lesson from `03ef9b61b` |
|#/one-the-ci-count |folded to `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/testing-architecture-scout` #/collided-2026-09-13 — <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/testing-architecture-scout">> `#/collided-2026-09-13`, beside the downstream copy already standing at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/rust-toolchain">> `#/open-loops` |
|#/two-the-terminal-compromise |folded to `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/recovery-custody-scout` #/our-own-ground — <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/recovery-custody-scout">> `#/our-own-ground` ③, with a pointer left at that carrier's `#/the-third-axis` so a reader meeting the spirit's quote reaches the measurement |
|#/three-the-edge-count |folded to `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/syncer-seams-roundtable` #/retirement — <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/syncer-seams-roundtable">> `#/retirement` — two inbound carriers across three `loulou` sites, the two welds named as different welds, and the code comment that re-points cheaply |
|#/four-the-ungoverned-floor |folded to `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/scale-stories-basket-one` — <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/scale-stories-basket-one">> — its `tags` and `status-why`, and the fold-readiness report inside the same carrier — plus `packages/lararium-tw5/tests/meme-lifecycle-tag.test.ts`, whose hold entry came out |
|#/the-crossings-row |folded to `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths` #/crossings — <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/hearths">> `#/crossings`, written verbatim once the deadlock that refused it dissolved |

!! Two things the enactment corrected in the handback itself

''① The suggested edit to `testing-architecture-scout` would have rewritten a Testimony-Verbatim fence.'' That carrier's `#/head` declares the register — //Nothing below is this house's own claim … kept whole, as a collision surface// — and the whole handoff rides inside a ````markdown fence. Replacing Mason's §3 prose in place, as this carrier proposed, would have destroyed the testimony to correct it. The enactment kept the fence ''byte-for-byte'' and stood the house's re-measurement BESIDE it at `#/collided-2026-09-13`, with a pointer from `#/head`. ''Law: a stale room inside a verbatim register gets a room next to it, never a rewrite — a testimony corrected in place has stopped being testimony, and the collision was the reason to keep it.''

''② `lifecycle/folded` was the wrong tag for `scale-stories-basket-one`, by the rite's own definition.'' This carrier offered `folded` or `standing`. `otakiage:233` requires of `folded`: ''zero'' inbound `loulou`, ''zero'' open leans, and every slot carrying a fold target — //Any of the three failing reads wrong and the carrier stays `standing`//. That floor carries SIX inbound carriers and no slot folded, so it takes `lifecycle/standing` with its witness sentence naming why. ''Law: a suggestion that offers two tags has not read the rite that grades them.''

''And one thing the enactment found that this carrier did not.'' Its item ④③ named ONE stale hold entry in `meme-lifecycle-tag.test.ts`. A second entry, `heraldry.mem`, stood equally stale and its carrier had ''already'' been swept at `58d47f09b` — so the file's own CONTROL stood RED against the tree before any of this landed, saying //swept; drop the entry//. Both came out together. ''Law: a hold entry carries two facts and its CONTROL can only check one'' — that the carrier still writes a retired key, never whether the REASON still describes the world; so a reason worded //this watch// outlives the watch while the entry still reads green.

!! What stands between this carrier and the fire

''One thing, and no hand here may supply it.'' `otakiage#/the-fire` rules the call: //the operator CALLS it; any hand may carry it// — the call and the carriage separate deliberately. Every other precondition stands measured:

* ''zero inbound `loulou`'' — `grep -rn "handback-stale-rooms" --include='*.mem' . | grep -v node_modules` returns one line, a bare prose mention inside the `hearths#/crossings` row, carrying no edge.
* ''zero open leans'' — every item enacted, each with its measurement re-derived.
* ''every slot carries a fold target'' — the table at #/fold.

''The two-commit shape the rite names'' puts this check-in and any delete in separate commits, so the shelf carries one state in which the carrier stands complete and marked as ending, with every pointer already re-aimed. This commit is that check-in.

